## The two findings

| Finding | Charge | File | Offending edge | severity / agent_risk |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| `crime_00002` | Layer Violation | `src/domain/billing.ts:2` | `domain/billing.ts` → `presentation/invoice-view.ts` (rule: `domain cannotImport presentation`) | 0.60 / 0.34 |
| `crime_00003` | Deep Import Abuse | `src/application/billing-flow.ts:4` | `some-pkg/dist/internal/private/helper.js` | 0.35 / 0.29 |

The full edge set in the fixture is only four imports, which makes the diagnosis unambiguous:

```
presentation/invoice-view.ts ──▶ application/billing-flow.ts   legal (no rule forbids it)
application/billing-flow.ts  ──▶ domain/billing.ts             legal
application/billing-flow.ts  ──▶ some-pkg/dist/internal/...    crime_00003
domain/billing.ts            ──▶ presentation/invoice-view.ts  crime_00002  ← the only illegal edge
infrastructure/db.ts                                            leaf, unimported
```

**The load-bearing observation:** `crime_00001` (the 3-file cycle) and `crime_00002` are the same edge. The ring is `billing-flow → billing → invoice-view → billing-flow`, and exactly one member of that ring points the wrong way against the layer rules — the domain→presentation edge. Deleting it collapses the cycle too. Two findings, one edit. That is not a coincidence: illegal back-edges are the usual mechanism by which cycles form, which is why `crimes` scores them separately but they resolve together.

## What to break, what to redirect

### 1. Break outright: `domain/billing.ts` → `presentation/invoice-view.ts` (`crime_00002`, and `crime_00001` with it)

`chargeCustomer` currently *returns a rendered string* — it does presentation work. The import isn't an accident of wiring; the function's return type is the violation. Fixing the import without fixing the signature just moves the problem.

Push the string formatting up and let domain return data:

```ts
// src/domain/billing.ts — no imports at all
export type Charge = { amount: number };
export function chargeCustomer(amount: number): Charge {
  return { amount };
}
```

```ts
// src/application/billing-flow.ts
import { chargeCustomer, type Charge } from "../domain/billing.js";
export function runBillingFlow(): Charge {
  return chargeCustomer(100);
}
```

```ts
// src/presentation/invoice-view.ts — keeps its existing (legal) import
import { runBillingFlow } from "../application/billing-flow.js";
export function renderInvoice(): string {
  const charge = runBillingFlow();
  return `Invoice: ${charge.amount}`;
}
```

Resulting graph is `presentation → application → domain`: acyclic, every edge legal under the config. Note that composing in `application` is *not* an option here — `crimes.config.json` also declares `application cannotImport presentation`, so `presentation` is the only layer allowed to be the composition root. That constraint is what picks this design.

The scan's `suggested_actions` offers an alternative — "move the cross-layer collaboration behind an interface owned by the domain layer." That's dependency inversion: declare an `InvoiceRenderer` port in `domain/` and inject an implementation. It's the right call *if* domain genuinely needs to trigger rendering as part of its own policy. It doesn't here — `chargeCustomer` just wants to hand back a number. Prefer returning data; reach for the port only when the domain must call out mid-operation.

### 2. Redirect, don't delete: `some-pkg/dist/internal/private/helper.js` (`crime_00003`)

`internalHelper` is presumably still needed, so this is a redirect:

```ts
import { internalHelper } from "some-pkg";   // if the symbol is on the public surface
```

If it isn't exported — likely, given the `internal/private/` path — don't code around it inline. Isolate it behind a single adapter so the illegal coupling has exactly one site:

```ts
// src/infrastructure/some-pkg-adapter.ts
// UPSTREAM: some-pkg does not export internalHelper. Tracked at <issue-url>.
// Re-verify on every some-pkg upgrade.
import { internalHelper } from "some-pkg/dist/internal/private/helper.js";
export function helper(): void { internalHelper(); }
```

`application → infrastructure` is permitted by the config, so `billing-flow.ts` imports the adapter and the deep specifier disappears from application code. This is a containment move, not a fix — `crimes` will still flag the adapter, correctly. Suppress it there with the upstream issue link as the receipt. Effort is `quick` and risk is `low`; do it independently of the layer work.

## How the two differ in agent risk

Their severities differ by 0.25 (0.60 vs 0.35) but their `agent_risk` differs by only 0.05 (0.34 vs 0.29). That compression is the interesting signal — `deep_import` punches well above its severity for an agent — and the two risks have different shapes:

**`layer_violation` is a wrong rule an agent will learn and repeat.** The code compiles, the tests pass, and nothing in `domain/billing.ts` looks wrong on its own — a reviewer reading only that file sees a two-line function calling a helper. The rule it breaks lives in `crimes.config.json`, a file an agent editing domain logic has no reason to open. So an agent extending `domain/` reads the existing import as precedent, infers "domain may call presentation here," and adds the second and third such import with full confidence. The damage compounds silently and is only detectable by a tool that reads the layer config. Its `confidence` is 0.95 because the rule is declared, not inferred — when a violation fires, it is a fact about the team's stated intent, not a heuristic. And this particular edge is already load-bearing: it's a member of `crime_00001`, so it has begun producing the second-order failures (re-entry bugs, unreliable tree-shaking) the cycle finding describes.

**`deep_import` is opaque coupling to something outside the repo.** It's locally *visible* — `internal/private/` in the specifier is a legible warning an agent can act on without external context, which is why the fix is `quick` and the action risk is `low`. It teaches no wrong rule about this codebase's structure. But an agent cannot reason about `internalHelper` at all: the symbol is in `node_modules`, its contract is undocumented, and nothing in-repo constrains its behavior. Any change an agent makes near it is made blind. The failure is temporal rather than architectural — it arrives at the next `some-pkg` upgrade, not at edit time — and it's loud when it lands (module resolution error), so it doesn't rot quietly. `confidence` is lower at 0.85 because the detector reasons from path shape, and some packages legitimately publish deep subpaths.

**Practical ordering:** fix `crime_00002` first — it's the one that changes what future agents believe about this codebase, and it retires `crime_00001` for free. `crime_00003` is a contained, well-signposted liability; fix it when convenient, and leave the upstream issue link in the adapter so the next agent knows it's deliberate rather than sloppy.

I've proposed the changes rather than applied them, per the scenario. Say the word and I'll make the edits and re-scan to confirm all three findings clear.
